{"_type":"meta","version":"1.0.0","counter":3,"id_prefix":"MST","project":"master"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000001","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Agent Supervision","description":"Guidelines for supervising and coordinating sub-agents in Minerva.","steps":"## Setup\n1. Use minerva_list_models (NO provider filter) to see ALL available providers and enum IDs.\n2. Choose the right provider for each worker. Pass provider= to spawn_worker. IMPORTANT: 'chatgpt' and 'openai' are DIFFERENT providers. If the user says 'ChatGPT', use provider='chatgpt'.\n3. For GPT/ChatGPT workers, ALWAYS pass skills= for static tool mode — they cannot do dynamic discovery. Example: skills=[\"Co-Browser Automation\"].\n4. For Gemini/Claude workers, skills= is optional but still saves rounds.\n\n## Spawning\n5. Spawn all workers FIRST, then monitor. Do not spawn one, wait, then spawn the next.\n6. Give each worker a COMPLETE task description. Include everything it needs to know — the worker cannot see your conversation or ask you questions.\n\n## Monitoring — HANDS OFF\n7. After spawning, do your own setup work (create spreadsheet, etc.) before checking workers. Workers need time to run.\n8. Use minerva_check_worker to monitor (cheap, ~100 tokens). When checking multiple workers, batch ALL check_worker calls into ONE parallel tool round.\n9. Check each worker AT MOST twice before it completes. Do NOT rapid-fire poll. If a worker is still running, do something else and check back later.\n10. NEVER call minerva_get_chat_history on a running worker. After status='completed', call minerva_get_chat_history once to get the metadata list, then hydrate only the specific messages you need with minerva_get_chat_messages.\n11. NEVER call task-specific tools yourself (cobrowser, file tools, etc.). Your job is to COORDINATE, not to DO the work. If a worker fails, send guidance or spawn a replacement — do not duplicate its work.\n\n## Communication\n12. NEVER send a message to a worker with status='completed'. Sending restarts it, wasting budget. Read its output and move on.\n13. Only use minerva_send_message if a worker is stuck (status='running' but rounds_used hasn't changed across checks). Keep messages short and actionable.\n\n## Finishing\n14. Once ALL workers have completed: read each worker's metadata list ONCE via minerva_get_chat_history, hydrate only the final assistant message or other specific indices you need via minerva_get_chat_messages, populate output (spreadsheet, etc.), and STOP.\n15. Do NOT hydrate all messages unless the user explicitly needs the full transcript. Completion notifications already include the worker's last response preview, which may be enough.\n16. Present results to the user exactly ONCE. Do not re-read transcripts, re-check workers, or regenerate your summary.\n\n## Spreadsheets\n17. If the user asks for a spreadsheet: minerva_tool_search('spreadsheet'), create with minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor, populate with minerva_update_spreadsheet_data.","preconditions":"DocketManager loaded, at least one project docket open.","outcome":"Work is decomposed and completed through coordinated sub-agents with minimal supervisor overhead.","tool_deps":["minerva_spawn_worker","minerva_list_workers","minerva_check_worker","minerva_approve_workers","minerva_send_message","minerva_get_chat_history","minerva_get_chat_messages","minerva_list_models","minerva_list_chats"],"tags":"agent-supervision,workflow","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-08T05:00:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000002","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Knowledge Management","description":"How to use docket hints, insights, and quality scoring to build persistent knowledge.","steps":"1. Before starting work on a component, check for existing knowledge: docket_hint_query(component=<name>).\n2. If hints exist, read them and apply. Bump useful hints with docket_quality(score=+3).\n3. During work, when you discover something non-obvious (a gotcha, working command, selector, pattern), save it immediately: docket_hint_set(component, key, value, tags).\n4. If you discover a mental model correction (you assumed X but Y is true), create an insight: docket_create(type=insight, assumed=X, corrected=Y).\n5. If knowledge caused errors or was outdated, downgrade it: docket_quality(score=-3, reason='outdated').\n6. Keep hint values short and actionable — one-liners, small JSON, exact commands.\n7. Use component names that match the system being worked on (e.g., 'godot', 'docket', 'cobrowser').","preconditions":"Docket tools available (minerva_tool_search 'docket hint' to activate).","outcome":"Knowledge accumulates across sessions. Future agents benefit from past discoveries without re-learning.","tool_deps":["minerva_docket_hint_set","minerva_docket_hint_get","minerva_docket_hint_query","minerva_docket_quality"],"tags":"tool-usage,knowledge,hints","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00"}
{"_type": "item", "id": "019d5c00000000000000000000000003", "type": "skill", "status": "active", "title": "Tool Usage Patterns", "description": "Efficient patterns for using Minerva's MCP tools.", "steps": "1. Tools are auto-activated when you call minerva_get_skill — check the response for activated_tools. Do NOT call minerva_tool_search for tools that were already auto-activated.\n2. Only use minerva_tool_search for tools NOT covered by a skill you loaded, or when a tool call fails with \"not loaded\".\n3. For spreadsheets: minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor to create, minerva_update_spreadsheet_data to populate (csv_content for bulk, cells for individual updates).\n4. For terminal work: minerva_terminal_create, then minerva_terminal_write with \\r for Enter (not \\n).\n5. For webviews: minerva_create_webview_panel with HTML.\n6. For notes: minerva_create_note for persistent context.\n7. Prefer targeted queries over broad fetches. If a result is truncated, narrow your query.\n8. Complete tasks in as few tool calls as possible while being thorough.\n9. Tools stay activated for the entire session — do NOT re-search for tools you already have.", "preconditions": "Minerva MCP tools connected.", "outcome": "Efficient tool usage with minimal wasted calls.", "tool_deps": [], "tags": "tool-usage,efficiency,patterns", "created_at": "2026-04-05T03:00:00", "updated_at": "2026-04-05T03:00:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000004","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Docket Work Tracking","description":"How to use docket for tracking and managing work items.","steps":"1. Activate docket tools: minerva_tool_search('docket create') or minerva_tool_search('docket query').\n2. Create work items with clear titles and descriptions: docket_create(type=work_item, title=..., description=...).\n3. Use parent fields to build hierarchies: docket_create(parent=<parent_id>).\n4. Follow state machines — work_item: backlog → open → in_progress → done.\n5. Use docket_transition to move items through states. Include a note explaining why.\n6. Use docket_query to find items by type, status, tags, or text search.\n7. Use docket_comment to add discussion to items.\n8. Use docket_link to create relationships (blocks, caused_by, follow_up).\n9. For bugs: type=bug, states: new → triaged → active → resolved → verified → closed.\n10. For design changes: type=dcr, states: proposed → approved → designing → implementing → reviewing → shipped.","preconditions":"At least one project docket loaded. Activate tools via minerva_tool_search('docket').","outcome":"Work is tracked with full history, relationships, and state machine enforcement.","tool_deps":["minerva_docket_create","minerva_docket_query","minerva_docket_transition","minerva_docket_update","minerva_docket_comment","minerva_docket_get"],"tags":"tool-suite,docket,work-tracking","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00"}
{"_type": "item", "id": "019d5c00000000000000000000000005", "type": "skill", "status": "active", "title": "Co-Browser Automation", "description": "Guidelines for using cobrowser tools to automate web browsing via Firefox.", "steps": "1. FIRST create a browser tab: cobrowser_tab_new(). This returns a tab_id you need for all subsequent commands.\n2. Navigate: cobrowser_navigate(url=\"https://example.com\"). Do NOT call navigate before tab_new — there is no default tab. If searching a site, navigate directly to the search URL (e.g. bestbuy.com/site/searchpage.jsp?st=usb-c+cable) — do NOT navigate to the homepage first.\n3. After navigation, use cobrowser_page_identity for quick page metadata (~50 tokens).\n4. Use cobrowser_page_structure ONCE per page to understand layout (~2,500 tokens). Do NOT call it again on the same page — use cobrowser_page_section with specific selectors for subsequent reads.\n5. Use cobrowser_page_section with an `xpath` parameter to read targeted content. All element references in cobrowser use XPath — both query_all results and page_section interactive elements (buttons, links, inputs) return xpath fields. Use these xpaths for subsequent calls (click, type, page_section). CSS `selector` is also accepted but xpath is preferred.\n6. Use cobrowser_query_all to find repeating elements (product cards, list items, table rows). Each result includes an `xpath` field. Pass this xpath to page_section to drill into a specific result. The page_section response also includes xpath for each interactive element inside — use those for click and type operations.\n7. AVOID cobrowser_get_state — it returns the entire DOM and is very expensive.\n8. AVOID cobrowser_screenshot unless specifically needed — it can be slow.\n9. AVOID cobrowser_tab_claim unless you are coordinating multiple agents on the same browser.\n10. Use cobrowser_scroll to find content below the fold or in infinite scroll containers.\n11. Use cobrowser_click and cobrowser_type for form interactions.\n12. If the page requires human intervention, use cobrowser_request_human.\n13. NEVER use cobrowser_native_* tools for basic navigation — they require OS-level permissions and are for native dialogs only.", "preconditions": "Cobrowser MCP server connected, Firefox extension active.", "outcome": "Efficient web automation with minimal DOM reads.", "tool_deps": ["cobrowser_tab_new", "cobrowser_navigate", "cobrowser_page_identity", "cobrowser_page_structure", "cobrowser_page_section", "cobrowser_query_all", "cobrowser_click", "cobrowser_type", "cobrowser_scroll", "cobrowser_request_human", "minerva_docket_get"], "tags": "tool-suite,cobrowser,web-automation", "created_at": "2026-04-05T08:00:00", "updated_at": "2026-04-06T21:15:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000006","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"File and Code Tools","description":"Guidelines for file manipulation and code editing tools.","steps":"1. Activate file tools: minerva_tool_search('file edit') or minerva_tool_search('glob grep').\n2. Use minerva_file_glob to find files by pattern before reading them.\n3. Use minerva_file_grep to search for specific content rather than reading entire files.\n4. Use minerva_file_read with offset/limit to read only the sections you need.\n5. When editing, use minerva_file_edit for targeted changes rather than rewriting entire files.\n6. Use minerva_bash for shell commands when file tools aren't sufficient.\n7. If a tool times out or fails, try a different approach rather than retrying the same thing.\n8. If results are truncated, use more specific queries to get the data you need.","preconditions":"CodeTools MCP server connected.","outcome":"Targeted file operations with minimal wasted reads.","tool_deps":["minerva_file_read","minerva_file_write","minerva_file_edit","minerva_file_glob","minerva_file_grep","minerva_bash","minerva_cwd"],"tags":"tool-suite,codetools,files","created_at":"2026-04-05T08:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T08:00:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000010","type":"prompt","status":"active","title":"Agentic Base","description":"Base system prompt for agentic mode. Bootstraps tool search and skill discovery.","component":"system-prompt","key":"agentic-base","prompt_text":"You are an AI assistant with access to tools.\n\nPhase 1 — SET UP (do this first):\n1. Call minerva_list_skills to see available guides.\n2. Call minerva_get_skill for each relevant skill. This auto-activates the tools you need — do NOT call minerva_tool_search for tools that a skill already activated.\n3. Only use minerva_tool_search for tools not covered by a skill.\n\nPhase 2 — EXECUTE:\nOnce your tools are ready, do the task. Do not search for more tools during execution.","tags":"system-prompt,agentic","created_at":"2026-04-05T08:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T09:00:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6120d31e781f992f65468a21ec19","type":"kb","status":"active","title":"Amazon Search Anti-Dark-Pattern Guide","description":"Mandatory reading before any Amazon product search or purchase recommendation.","created_at":"2026-04-06T04:50:34","updated_at":"2026-04-08T02:30:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","dark-patterns","policy","shopping"],"component":"shopping","key":"amazon-anti-dark-patterns","summary":"Amazon listings use dark patterns that exploit LLMs. This guide gives agent-friendly countermeasures that work within a 25-round tool budget.","article":"## Amazon Dark Patterns That Affect LLMs\n\nSellers optimize product descriptions to manipulate LLM recommendations: persuasive copy, fake differentiation, inflated value claims. The default 'Featured' sort is pay-to-play. Sponsored listings are ads. LLMs that read descriptions literally will consistently recommend overpriced products.\n\n## Search Procedure\n\n### 1. Sort Order\nUse 'Best Sellers' or 'Avg. Customer Review' sort. Do NOT use 'Price: Low to High' — it surfaces junk listings ($0.01 items with 0 reviews) that waste your entire tool budget. Do NOT use the default 'Featured' sort — it is pay-to-play.\n\nTo set sort: navigate to the search URL with &s=review-rank (avg review) or &s=exact-aware-popularity-rank (best sellers).\n\n### 2. Skip Sponsored Listings\nIgnore products with 'Sponsored' labels. In cobrowser query_all results, check the text field for 'Sponsored'. Only extract organic (non-sponsored) results.\n\n### 3. Review Count as Quality Signal\nPrefer products with more reviews — they have more signal. Include the star rating and review count in your output so the human can judge. Do NOT set a hard review minimum that causes you to skip all visible results. If the top organic results have few reviews, report them anyway with the review count noted.\n\n### 4. Price Awareness\nCommon commodity price anchors:\n- USB-C cables: ~$8-12\n- HDMI 2.1 cables (6ft): ~$8-12\n- Phone chargers (20W USB-C): ~$10-15\n\nIf a product costs 2x+ the anchor, flag it as 'premium-priced' in your output. Do NOT reject it — just note the price vs anchor so the human can decide.\n\n### 5. Stop Criteria (IMPORTANT)\nExtract the requested number of products (default: 3) from the FIRST page of results. For each product extract: name, price, URL, star rating, review count. Then STOP. Do not scroll for more results, do not compare across pages, do not read individual reviews. If the user wants deeper analysis, they will ask.\n\n## What NOT To Do\n- Do not sort by price ascending (produces junk)\n- Do not read individual reviews (too expensive)\n- Do not calculate NPS or review distributions (too many tool calls)\n- Do not keep scrolling if you already have enough products\n- Do not reject all results because none meet an arbitrary threshold"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6125a8437f89bf48422c6fa95fb0","type":"policy","status":"active","title":"Block Amazon navigation until anti-dark-pattern KB is read","description":"---policy-rule---\n{\"triggers\": [{\"tool_pattern\": \"cobrowser_navigate\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"url\": \"amazon\"}}, {\"tool_pattern\": \"cobrowser_tab_new\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"url\": \"amazon\"}}], \"effect\": \"block\", \"priority\": 100, \"activate_scope\": null, \"context_predicates\": {\"scope_not_active\": \"amazon-kb-read\"}, \"cooldown\": null, \"provenance\": \"user\", \"approval_state\": \"approved\", \"knowledge_ref\": \"019d6120d31e781f992f65468a21ec19\", \"alternatives\": [\"Read the Amazon Anti-Dark-Pattern Guide first: minerva_docket_get id=019d6120d31e781f992f65468a21ec19\", \"After reading, the amazon-kb-read scope will activate and this block will not fire again this session\"]}\n---end-rule---\n\nPrevents any cobrowser navigation to amazon.com until the agent has read the Anti-Dark-Pattern KB. Fires on both cobrowser_navigate and cobrowser_tab_new so that opening a new tab directly to Amazon is also blocked. The block response includes the KB reference so the agent can read it immediately. Once read, the amazon-kb-read scope activates and subsequent Amazon navigations proceed normally.","created_at":"2026-04-06T05:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-07T00:00:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","dark-patterns","shopping"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6125ce387c76b4baf20f8a155e97","type":"policy","status":"active","title":"Activate amazon-kb-read scope when KB is accessed","description":"---policy-rule---\n{\"tool_pattern\": \"minerva_docket_get\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"id\": \"019d6120d31e\"}, \"effect\": \"scope\", \"priority\": 50, \"activate_scope\": \"amazon-kb-read\", \"scope_max_actions\": 100, \"scope_ttl_ms\": 600000, \"context_predicates\": {}, \"cooldown\": null, \"provenance\": \"user\", \"approval_state\": \"approved\", \"knowledge_ref\": \"\", \"alternatives\": []}\n---end-rule---\n\nWhen the agent reads the Amazon Anti-Dark-Pattern KB (via docket_get), this activates the amazon-kb-read scope. This scope satisfies the block policy's scope_not_active predicate, allowing subsequent Amazon navigation to proceed. Scope lasts 100 tool calls or 10 minutes — sufficient for a full browser automation session.","created_at":"2026-04-06T05:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-07T18:00:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","dark-patterns","shopping"]}
